{"name":"Green Fog","hex":"#989a87","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"fog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick cloud that forms near the ground; the obscurity of such a cloud.","A mist or film clouding a surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with or as if with fog.","To become obscured in condensation or water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog","wikidata":"Q37477"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Minute droplets of water constitute this after-dark radiation fog, with an ambient temperature of −2 °C (28 °F). Their motion trails are captured as streaks.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A close-up view of water droplets forming fog. Those outside the camera lens's depth of field appear as orbs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/1280px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Ground fog in Frankfurt, Germany.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg/500px-San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg/1280px-San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg/500px-San_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Advection fog layer in San Francisco with the Golden Gate Bridge and skyline in the background","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASan_francisco_in_fog_with_rays.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Sydney_Harbour_Fog_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-Sydney_Harbour_Fog_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_cont